IV Runs 2 and 3 — v1.0.0 — 30 September 2026

Purpose and scope

Start the requested work from the existing Mac caches. This package does not
contain your private data and cannot produce empirical results on this host.

RUN3_LENIENCY.command runs the subgroup first stages and instrument-weighted
complier descriptions on the verified spaced90.parquet sample. It refreshes the
necessary score/reference/recipient histories under the author exclusion.
The formal Frandsen–Lefgren–Leslie test remains UNRUN: the original packages do
not provide a valid mapping from this dynamic pair-leave-all-out score to that
test's assignment instruments and inference. No substitute test is mislabeled FLL.

RUN2_RESUME.command continues the existing fixed comparison metadata download.
It is NOT an expanded placebo estimator. The original download package produces
metadata only; the expanded frame still needs identity/cache reconciliation and
an analysis adapter. Its small return report makes that boundary explicit.
Do not reuse the old 1/1024 estimate and call it the new Run 2 result.

The immediate submission priority—first stage by speed, recipient rating bin,
month, and recipient prior granting—is fully implemented. Run 3 does not gate
submission. Run 2 continues to gate the abstract sentence and “Contagious”.

Expected runtime

* Run 3: roughly 1–3 hours initially on a fast laptop with an SSD. This is a
  planning estimate, not a measured run on your data. The 47.6M-row narrow history
  caches are reused; no raw PGNs, engine searches, or rating replay. Hash checks
  and score reproduction may dominate. Completed stages are reusable.
* Run 2 status: usually under 30 seconds.
* Run 2 download: remaining IDs / observed throughput, plus retries and export.
  At preparation there were 57,087,753 targets. At the verified 29.05 games/s,
  that is 545.9 hours (22.75 days). The earlier “two days” handoff estimate is not
  supported by the preparation report. The checkpoint may already remove much
  of this work. The runner prints actual remaining work and a live ETA.
* Synthetic tests: about 5–30 seconds on a laptop. No live API calls.

Run on the Mac

Place IV_Runs_2_3_v1_0_0.zip in ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop, verify the supplied
SHA256, and extract it there with ditto. Then:

  bash "$HOME/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/IV_Runs_2_3_v1_0_0/RUN3_LENIENCY.command"

In another terminal, after the current API collector has finished:

  bash "$HOME/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/IV_Runs_2_3_v1_0_0/RUN2_RESUME.command"

Or inspect progress at any time:

  bash "$HOME/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/IV_Runs_2_3_v1_0_0/STATUS_RUN2.command"

Recognizable existing collectors are detected and waited for automatically.
If a separately launched collector uses an unknown script name, wait for it to
finish before running Run 2, or set IV_WAIT_PID to its PID. No downloader can
guarantee discovery of an arbitrary unrelated process. Run 3 is local and can
run alongside an API collector.

Defaults

Project: /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness
Python: /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/venv/bin/python
Desktop: ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop
No SciPy, pandas, pip installation, or change to the existing environment.
Uses NumPy, DuckDB and PyArrow already present in your environment.
Up to 10 processes; CPU/RAM detection reduces workers when required. Each worker
has one BLAS thread. The largest pooled fit runs separately to avoid overlapping
full-cohort memory copies. DuckDB spills and Zstandard Parquet are used throughout.
Optional overrides: IV_PROJECT_ROOT, IV_PYTHON, IV_DESKTOP, IV_WORKERS,
IV_MEMORY_GB. RAM is an approximate working allowance, not an OS hard limit.

Run 3 design

Input: verified completed 24-month spaced90.parquet, 2,682,330 original rows.
This is a diagnostic on the existing frozen cohort, not the 32-month extension.
Original spacing stays fixed. Author-involving rows are excluded without topping
up the cohort; rows losing score support are counted explicitly.

The author identities __PRIVATE_VALUE_1__ and __PRIVATE_VALUE_2__ are resolved through the original
verified dictionary and its selected numeric-ID field. Both roles are excluded
from score histories and nuisance reference data. Recipient behavioral counts
are adjusted on the identical lagged windows. Factual rating/activity history
is retained. The original scores are reproduced before any clean score is used.
This local refresh covers these diagnostics; it does not complete the separate
project-wide author refresh for every manuscript analysis.

Groups, frozen before new first-stage estimates:
  Speed: ultrabullet, bullet, blitz, rapid, classical.
  Recipient displayed rating: below 1200; 1200–1599; 1600–1999; 2000+.
  Exposure month: all 24 source months (empty/ineligible months are explicit).
  Recipient prior granting: no fair decision; fair decisions but no grants;
    at least one grant in [exposure start − 97 days, exposure start − 7 days).
  Overall: one pooled first stage, not a headline IV reestimate.

Each group has raw and adjusted first stages. Adjusted controls and fixed effects
are the existing main90 rich specification, refit within subgroup. Inference uses
the existing shared-account OR exposure-date CR0 covariance and normal intervals.
The historical reference SD is 0.059411690344000595 for every reported pp effect.
N, receipts, distinct recipients/choosers, residual score SD, F, partial R² and
negative/reversal flags accompany each result. Fewer than 2,000 rows or fewer than
20 receipts/nonreceipts gives an explicit unsupported result. The one-sided
negative-first-stage p-values receive a Holm correction across all 36 subgroups.
Positive first stages do not establish individual monotonicity.

Complier means use the ratio sum(z_residual * D * X) / sum(z_residual * D), with
the same model residualization and clustered ratio variance. They describe the
continuous instrument's weighted treatment margin under maintained conditional
IV assumptions, not an observed discrete complier population. Negative weights
and out-of-range means are reported without clipping. Scores are treated as fixed
regressors, consistent with the prior analysis; this does not propagate all
generated-score uncertainty or wider network dependence. Recipient outcome Y is
not read by Run 3. Nonpositive covariance is reported without repair.

Run 2 boundaries and manuscript use

61,158,951 is the size of the fixed 1/32 frame itself. All endings are retained
for the entry denominator. The narrow ordinary placebo uses mate/resign/stalemate;
broader draws are a separate sensitivity. The fixed sample cannot be stopped or
expanded based on an interim estimate.

The new IV-recipient-restricted placebo is secondary because membership in the
eventual IV cohort can condition on future behavior. It must not replace the
prospective entry test. Identity, source, nonadjacency, history, timing and author
exclusion must be harmonized before the expanded estimates are computed.

The historical precision plan targets SE about 0.005 pp per reference SD, with
±0.015 pp equivalence assessed by two one-sided 5% tests (90% CI). The handoff's
±0.005 point-estimate rule is not an equivalence test. A narrow point estimate
alone cannot validate exclusion. Report the estimate and interval beside the
roughly 0.020386 pp/SD direct-effect sensitivity number.

Until those estimates exist, retain the IV in the main text with the v2 wording:
the placebo is centered near zero and its interval includes a value large enough
to matter. Neither download completion nor Run 3 clears the abstract/title gate.

Reports to return

IV_RUN3_LENIENCY_REPORT_<hash>.zip contains aggregate first-stage and complier
tables (CSV, JSON, Parquet), design, sample-flow, runtime and formal-test status.
IV_RUN2_DOWNLOAD_STATUS_628dfb8f80a8.zip contains download accounting and the
remaining analysis requirements. The private checkpoint database and individual
game/account Parquets stay on the Mac. Logs are written in Lichess_Desktop.

